(Aurelia Maren wrote a series of letters between 1942 and 1944 arguing that duty) Tj
T*
(must balance compassion. In a 1943 letter from Lisbon, she praised her mentor) Tj
T*
(Elias Nyberg for teaching her to "oppose cruelty even when the law approves it.") Tj
T*
(She also referenced the essay "On Mercy" by scholar Laila Ben-Youssef, saying it) Tj
T*
(shaped her beliefs more than any court ruling.) Tj
T*
T*
(During the 1944 March Uprising in Porto, Aurelia sheltered refugees. She later) Tj
T*
(testified that Colonel Mateo Ruiz ordered the blockade and that she disobeyed him.) Tj
T*
(This event marked a break with her former ally, the Civic Order Council, which she) Tj
T*
(publicly criticized afterward.) Tj
T*
T*
(In 1945, Aurelia's speech "After the Smoke" contrasted the concept of obedience) Tj
T*
(with the concept of conscience. She claimed she was influenced by her late) Tj
T*
(brother, Tomas Maren, who had refused to carry out expulsions in 1940. She cited) Tj
T*
(Elias Nyberg again as "the teacher who made me see obedience as a tool, not a) Tj
T*
(purpose.") Tj
T*
T*
(By 1947, Aurelia founded the Harbor Circle, a small group that met in Porto and) Tj
T*
(Madrid. The Circle discussed themes of amnesty, repair, and duty. Minutes from the) Tj
T*
(12 June 1947 meeting show Aurelia opposing Colonel Ruiz's policies, while Laila) Tj
T*
(Ben-Youssef advocated negotiation. The group planned a petition that winter urging) Tj
T*
(the Civic Order Council to reverse the blockade doctrine.) Tj
